ant"],["Bayonne ham","dish/bayonne-ham"],["Be Our Guest Restaurant","dish/be-our-guest-restaurant"],["Beaulieu, Doorwerth Castle","dish/beaulieu-doorwerth-castle"],["Beef Stroganoff","dish/beef-stroganoff"],["Bell's (restaurant)","dish/bell-s-restaurant"],["Belmond Le Manoir aux Quat'Saisons","dish/belmond-le-manoir-aux-quat-saisons"],["Beluga (restaurant)","dish/beluga-restaurant"],["Berowra Waters Inn","dish/berowra-waters-inn"],["Beurre maître d'hôtel","dish/beurre-maitre-d-hotel"],["Beurre manié","dish/beurre-manie"],["Bisque (food)","dish/bisque-food"],["Bistro 990","dish/bistro-990"],["Bistro Agnes","dish/bistro-agnes"],["Bistro Jeanty","dish/bistro-jeanty"]]}]},"dish/concha":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/concha","n":"Concha","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Mexican cuisine","dot":"#94bd5c","intro":[["A concha is a traditional Mexican sweet bread (pan dulce) with similar consistency to a brioche. Conchas get their name from their round shape and their striped, seashell-like appearance. A concha consists of two parts: a sweetened bread roll, and a crunchy topping (composed of flour, butter, and sugar), the most common topping flavors being chocolate, vanilla, and strawberry. Conchas are commonly found throughout Mexico, Guatemala, and their diasporas in panaderias (bakeries). They can also be found in grocery stores and bakeries across the United States."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-concha"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Concha"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Mexican cuisine","cuisine/mexican-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Mexico in My Kitchen","ref":"site/mexicoinmykitchen-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"mexicoinmykitchen.com","note":"Mely Martínez's long-running blog of Mexican home-style recipes.","url":"https://www.mexicoinmykitchen.com/","suggested":true},{"name":"Pati Jinich","ref":"site/patijinich-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub